readings.c1s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Nitella . . . was always in a ''wrapper'', nightcap, and slippers when she was not decorated for immediate show.
{{right|''Johnson'', Rambler, No. 115.}}}}
{{smaller block|Similar mantles, not assumed as ''wrappers'' for extra warmth or protection against the weather, were in general use at ceremonies and festivals.{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', VI. 465.}}}}
{{smaller block|She wore a dismal calico ''wrapper'', which made no compromise with the gauntness of her figure.
{{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVII. 137.}}}}
'''4.''' An undershirt. [Colloq. or trade use.] —'''5.''' In ''Fungi'', same as ''volva''.

'''wrapping-paper''' (rap′ing-pā″pėr), ''n.'' See ''paper''.

'''wrapping-silk''' (rap′ing-silk), ''n.'' See ''silk''.

'''wrap-rascal''' (rap′rȧs″kal), ''n.'' [< ''wrap'' + obj. ''rascal''; a humorous term, like ''hap-harlot''.] A loose greatcoat worn by people of elegance about 1740, in supposed imitation of the coarse coats of the poorer people; hence, any surtout or long outer garment.
{{smaller block|His dress was also that of a horse-dealer — a close-buttoned jockey-coat, or ''wrap-rascal'', as it was then termed, with huge metal buttons, coarse blue upper stockings, called boot-hose, because supplying the place of boots, and a slouched hat.{{right|''Scott'', Heart of Mid-Lothian, xiii.}}}}
{{smaller block|The driver, by means of a ''wraprascal'', had covered a great part of the rags of his lower garment.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Irish Sketch-Book, xix.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet in his ''wraugth'' this thought he euer among:
If he shuld avenge hym sodenly,
All his pepill wold say he did hym wrong.</poem>
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1373.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wraththe'' of children is ouercome soone.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 31.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Then boyling ''Wrath'', stern, cruell, swift, and rash,
That like a Boar her teeth doth grinde and gnash.</poem>
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas’s Weeks, ii., The Furies.}}}}

'''2†.''' Heat; impetuosity.
{{smaller block|They are in the very ''wrath'' of love, and they will together; clubs cannot part them.
{{right|''Shak.'', As you Like it, v. 2. 44.}}}}

'''3.''' The effects of anger; the just punishment of an offense or crime; vengeance. Rom. xiii. 4. —'''To pour out vials of wrath.''' {{smaller|See ''vial''. ='''Syn. 1.''' ''Anger'', ''Vexation'', ''Indignation'', etc. (see ''anger''<sup>1</sup>).}}

'''wrath†''' (räth), ''a.'' An obsolete (in early modern use erroneous) form of ''wroth''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Whereat the Prince full ''wrath'' his strong right hand
In full avengement heaved up on hie.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. viii. 43.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Oberon is passing fell and ''wrath''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., ii. 1. 20.}}}}

'''wrath†''' (räth), ''v.'' [< ME. ''wraththen'', ''wratthen'', ''wrathen'', ''wrathien'', < AS. ''gewrāthian'' (= OS. ''wrēthian'' = Icel. ''reitha''), be angry, < ''wrāth'', angry: see ''wroth'' and ''wrath'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To become wroth or angry; manifest anger.
{{smaller block|<poem>Than the worthy at his wife ''wrathet'' a little,
And blamyt the burde for hir bold speche.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 8442.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And appere in hus presence whyle hym pleye lyketh,
And yf he ''wratthe'', we mowe be war and hus way roume.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), i. 189.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To make wroth or angry; cause

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|him, and with his owne propre Swerd he was slayn.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 37.}}
I wol not ''wrathe'' him, also mote I thryve.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Manciple’s Tale, l. 80.}}
And that es drede perfite in vs and gastely when we drede to ''wrethe'' God in the leste syne that we kane knawe and flese it als venyme.
{{right|''Hampole'', Prose Treatises (E. E. T. S.), p. 12.}}}}
'''2.''' To be angry with; exhibit anger or wrath to.
{{smaller block|<poem>Whi ''wraththist'' thou me? y greue thee nouȝt.
Whi art thou to thi freend vnkinde?</poem>
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 161.}}}}

¶
'''wrathful''' (räth′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wrethful'', ''wrethvol'', wrathful; < ''wrath'', ''n.'', + ''-ful''.] '''1.''' Full of wrath; very angry; greatly incensed.
{{smaller block|<poem>Strong men, and ''wrathful'' that a stranger knight
Should do and almost overdo the deeds
Of Lancelot.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}
'''2.''' Expressive of or prompted or characterized by wrath or anger; raging; impetuous; furious: as, ''wrathful'' passions; a ''wrathful'' countenance.
{{smaller block|<poem>How now, lords! your ''wrathful'' weapons drawn
Here in our presence?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 2. 237.}}
Like Lightning, swift the ''wrathful'' Faulchion flew.
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, x. 524.}}}}
'''3.''' Executing wrath; serving as the instrument of wrath. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Whiles we, God’s ''wrathful'' agent, do correct
Their proud contempt that beats His peace to heaven.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, ii. 1. 87.}}}}

readings.c2s2:
='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|Indignant, resentful, exasperated, irate.}}

'''wrathfully''' (räth′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''wrethfully''; < ''wrathful'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a wrathful manner; with anger; angrily.
{{smaller block|Then thes Paynymes ''wrethfully'' ther thens<br>Whent, leuying anon ther stourdy uiolens.
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2218.}}
Kill him boldly, but not ''wrathfully.''
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., ii. 1. 172.}}}}

'''wrathfulness''' (räth′fu̇l-nes), ''n.'' The character or state of being wrathful; vehement anger.

'''wrathily''' (rä′thi-li), ''adv.'' [< ''wrathy'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] With wrath or great anger; angrily. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|The master ''wrathily'' insisted.
{{right|''G. W. Cable'', Old Creole Days, Posson Jone.}}}}

'''wrathless''' (räth′les), ''a.'' [< ME. ''wraththelees''; < ''wrath'', ''n.'', + ''-less''.] Free from anger. ''Waller'', Of the Countess of Carlisle’s Chamber.

'''wrathy''' (rä′thi), ''a.'' [< ''wrath'', ''n.'', + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Angry. [Colloq.]

'''wraw†''', ''a.'' [ME. ''wraw'', ''wrah'', ''wroȝ'', pl. ''wrowe'', perverse, angry, fierce; cf. ''wro'', a corner.] Angry; froward; peevish.
{{smaller block|With this speche the cook wex wroth and ''wraw.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Prol. to Manciple’s Tale, l. 46.}}}}

'''wrawful†''', ''a.'' [ME., < ''wraw'' + ''-ful''.] Peevish; angry.
{{smaller block|Ire troubleth a man, and accidie maketh hym hevy, thoghtful, and ''wrawful.''
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parson’s Tale.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''wrawl†''', ''v. i.'' [Prob. a var. of ''wawl'', ''waul''.] To cry as a cat; waul; whine; moan.

{{smaller block|<poem>Nor practize snuffingly to speake, for that doth imitate
The brutish Storke and Elephant, yea, and the ''wralling'' cat.</poem>
{{right|''Babees Book'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 293.}}
Cats that ''wrawling'' still did cry.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. xii. 27.}}}}

'''wrawness†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''wrawnesse'', perverseness, peevishness; < ''wraw'' + ''-ness''.] Anger; peevishness; frowardness.

{{smaller block|He dooth alle thyng with anoy, and with ''wrawnesse'', slaknesse, and excusacioun.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Parson’s Tale.}}}}

'''wraxling''' (raks′ling), ''a.'' A dialectal form of ''wrastling'' for ''wrestling''. ''Davies.'' [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|As long as there ’s a devil or devils, even an ass or asses, in the universe, one will have to turn out to the reveille now and then, wherever one is, and satisfy one’s θυμός, rage, or pluck, which Plato averreth (for why, he’d have been a ''wraxling'' man, and therefore was a philosopher, and the king of ’em) to be the root of all virtue.
{{right|''C. Kingsley'', Life, II. 53. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''wray†''' (rā), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''wreyen'', ''wreien'', ''wreȝen'', < AS. ''wrēgan'' = OS. ''wrōgian'' = OFries. ''wrōgia'' = OHG. ''ruogen'' = Icel. ''rægja'' = Goth. ''wrōhjan'', accuse, betray. Cf. ''bewray''.] '''1.''' To reveal; disclose.

{{smaller block|<poem>Thou shalt upon thy trouthe swere me heere
That to no wight thou shalt this conseil ''wreye''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 317.}}
The work ''wrayes'' the man.
{{right|''Mir. for Mags.'', p. 82. (''Nares.'')}}}}

readings.c3s1:
''wroken, wroke, wreke''), < AS. ''wrecan'' (pret. ''wræc'', pp. ''wrecen''), wreak, revenge, punish, orig. drive, urge, impel, = OS. ''wrecan'' = OFries. ''wreka'' = D. ''wreken'', repel, toss, also wreak vengeance, = OHG. ''rehhan'', MHG. ''rechen'', G. ''rächen'', revenge, etc., = Icel. ''reka'' (for ''vreka''), drive, thrust, repel, toss, also wreak, = Sw. ''vräka'', reject, refuse, throw, = Dan. ''vrage'', reject, = Goth. ''wrikan'', persecute, ''ga-wrikan'', avenge; cf. Lith. ''wargti'', suffer affliction, ''wargas'', affliction, OBulg. Russ. ''vragŭ'', enemy, foe, persecutor; L. ''vergere'', bend, turn, incline (see ''verge''<sup>2</sup>), ''urgere'', press, urge (see ''urge''), Gr. εἴργειν, repel, Skt. √ ''varj'', turn, twist.] '''1.''' To revenge; avenge: with either the offense or the person offended as the object. [Obsolescent.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Now tyme, by my trauthe, to take it on hond,
To mene vs with manhode & our mys ''wreke''.</poem>
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 1750.}}
<poem>Thogh his bowe be nat broken,
He wol nat with his arwes been ''ywroken''
On thee ne me, ne noon of oure figure.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Envoy of Chaucer to Scogan, l. 26.}}
To send down Justice for to ''wreak'' our wrongs.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tit. And., iv. 3. 51.}}
<poem>Grant me some knight to do the battle for me,
Kill the foul thief, and ''wreak'' me for my son.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

'''2.''' To execute; inflict: as, to ''wreak'' vengeance on an enemy.
{{smaller block|Working that malice on the creatures heere, which he could not there so easily ''wrecke'' on their Creator.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 25.}}
On me let Death ''wreak'' all his rage.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iii. 241.}}}}
